module sh.sysl.ui

import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// State, and the two ways anything finds out that it changed.
//
// **This is what stands in for a reconciler.** Diffing an erased tree would need to ask a node what
// type it is, and `02 § Object safety` refuses that: there is no way back from an erasure. Fine-
// grained reactivity sidesteps the question rather than answering it -- a write notifies what read,
// nothing walks the tree, and nothing needs to know a node's type. SolidJS, Svelte 5, Angular and
// Swift Observation have all landed here, from four different starting points.
//
// **There are two mechanisms here and they answer different questions.** `writes()` is a count of
// every write anybody has made, and it is what a frame loop and a `Derived` both watch: it cannot be
// forgotten and it says nothing about *what* changed. `watch` is the per-signal dependent list,
// which says exactly what changed and has to be remembered. The first is what an application should
// use; the second is what a fine-grained rebuild would need, and card 0203 asked whether it works.

// How many times anything anywhere has been written.
//
// **One counter for the whole application, and it is what replaces remembering to subscribe.** A
// frame loop that compares it against what it last saw rebuilds when any state has changed, whatever
// that state is and however much of it there is -- where a `watch` per signal is a line per signal,
// and the one somebody forgets is a control that draws correctly and silently never updates. That is
// the worst failure a user interface has, because nothing reports it.
//
// It is also what `Derived` decides freshness by; `atom.sysl` argues that at length.
//
// **It is a bare module-level `var` and it must stay one, which is a portability constraint rather
// than a style.** It was a `&Epoch` -- a boxed counter with a `bump` method -- until an Android
// build refused the whole toolkit:
//
//     'sdl_main' is exported and reaches 'sh.sysl.ui.clock', which is module storage an initializer
//     fills before the program's own statements run. A C project linking this supplies its own
//     'main', so nothing fills it and the function would read whatever the loader left.
//
// A program built by `sysl build-c` -- which is every Android app, every Pico program and anything
// else whose link belongs to CMake or Gradle -- has no sysl `main` to run module initializers, so
// **computed** module storage is unreachable from an `@export`ed function. Storage whose initializer
// is constant data is laid straight into the object file and is fine, which is what this is.
//
// **Nothing in the package's own test suite can catch a regression here**, because `sysl test` links
// a sysl program and runs the initializers: what catches it is a `sysl build-c` of a program that
// uses the toolkit, and `sysl-lang/syslui-android` is that program.
private var clock: int = 0

// How many writes have happened. Compare it against what you last saw.
writes() -> int = clock

// A value that says who read it, so a write can tell exactly them.
//
// **A dependent is held weakly, and that is the whole lifetime story.** A subtree that has been
// dropped must stop being notified, and the alternative to a weak edge is an explicit unsubscribe
// on every teardown path -- which is a leak waiting for the one path somebody forgets. `weak Fn()`
// makes the drop itself the unsubscribe.
//
// Note the spelling: **`weak &Fn()` is refused**, because `weak` already implies the counted box.
//
//     'weak Fn() -> unit' is already a weak edge to a counted Fn() -> unit, so the '&' says the
//     mode a second time -- write 'weak Fn() -> unit'
struct Signal[T]
    value: T

    // Everything that has read this value and wants telling when it changes.
    //
    // Empty slots accumulate as subtrees go, and are swept on the next notify rather than eagerly:
    // there is nothing to hook a drop onto from this side, which is the cost of holding the edge
    // weakly rather than the other way round.
    deps: Buf[weak Fn() -> unit]

    // Read the value and register the caller as a dependent.
    //
    // **A read registers nothing, and that is now a decision rather than a shortcoming.** This file
    // used to say a finished framework would keep a stack of "who is currently building" so that a
    // plain read subscribed the reader. It would -- and `writes()` gets the same result for an
    // application without one, because the tree is rebuilt whole anyway. The tracking stack is what
    // a *fine-grained* rebuild needs, and it is worth building the day there is one.
    read(self) -> T = self.value

    // Register `f` to be called whenever this changes.
    //
    // **Nothing in the toolkit uses this any more, and it is kept deliberately.** It is card 0203's
    // third question — whether `weak Fn() -> unit` is a working dependent list whose drop is the
    // unsubscribe — and it is the mechanism a *fine-grained* rebuild would need, where only the
    // subtree that read a signal is built again. What replaced it for the ordinary case is
    // `writes()`, which cannot be forgotten; what it still answers is a question about the
    // architecture, and its two tests are that answer.
    watch(*self, f: &Fn() -> unit)
        self.deps.push(f)

    // Write, tell everything still alive that read, and move the clock.
    //
    // **The clock moves whether or not anything is watching**, which is what makes `writes()` a
    // complete account of whether state has changed. A signal nothing subscribed to is still state.
    set(*self, v: T)
        self.value = v
        clock += 1
        self.notify()

    // Call every live dependent, and forget the ones that have gone.
    //
    // **A weak reference is reached only through the `Option` a promotion hands back**, which is
    // what makes "a dependent never dangles" a fact about the type rather than a discipline. A
    // subtree dropped between two writes is simply not called, and its slot does not survive the
    // sweep.
    notify(*self)
        var live: Buf[weak Fn() -> unit] = buf()
        val v = self.deps.view()

        for i in 0..<v.len
            v[i].get() match
                Some(f) ->
                    f()
                    live.push(f)
                None -> ()

        self.deps = live
    end notify
end Signal

signal[T](v: T) -> &Signal[T] = Signal(v, buf())
